A||||4||theogre||Brake Upgrade/Fluid, round I don't know any more.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||06-28-2001||12:36 AM||||Anyone considering the "full" front/rear grand am upgrade should read all the documents at <A HREF="http://www.baer.com" TARGET=_blank>www.baer.com</A>  (You will need Acrobat Reader for some of the most important documents. The big one is the application guide.)<P>I'll be adding their information to my cave in the future. I'm still digesting it right now... I'm probably going to be talking to them in the not distant future.<P>Baer does nothing but brakes. They make brake systems for things like Indy cars. The info they have published completely disagrees with the "Grand Am upgrade" poeple. (They are also another brake maker that Does Not recomend Silicone brake fluid in street or race driven cars.)<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||06-28-2001||09:22 AM||||I have a 1951 Chevy truck that I had been using DOT 5 fluid in the brake system and it always seemed to have a problem with sponginess and high pedal effort.<P>When I rebuilt the master cylinder, I switched back to DOT 4 and it works so much better that I'll never go back. <P>DOT 3/4 is also much  cheaper than DOT 5.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||06-28-2001||09:52 AM||||5 is meant for newer braking systems , it has a higher boiling point. Doesn;t help normal street cars brake ...but when it comes down to running your brakes hot in competition dot 5 is an asset. I have boiled DOt 4 before in the fiero , not fun.<P>IF your just a street / performance guy stick with a good dot 4 fluid , Motul makes some nice stuff. Going to be running real hard , get dot 5 <P><BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||06-28-2001||09:00 PM||||Baer carries Castrol race brake fuild as their prefered high temp product. They recomend it for all race applications. I don't know much about it. That product isn't listed in Castrol's web sites that I could find it. They have another fluid they recomend for most vehicles. Wilwood also has a high temp fluid they recomend over Silicone.<P>Valvoline makes a synthetic fluid that I've mentioned before. It's dry boil temp is directly competitive with Silicone fluids yet it retains Dot 3/4 compatibility. (513F for valvoline synth vs. about 500F for Silicone.) This is the fluid I use in everything now. <P>Since Fiero doesn't have it, we don't often get into the details of ABS, you can't use Silicone in those systems either.<P>The big concerns with Si in Fiero are the flow valve in the brake MC and the small passages in the balance block. Si fluid has problems with small openings. That's part of why you can't use it in ABS. In a stock setup, those items mainly effect the rear brakes. Lossing the rear brakes is bad, but not as bad as loosing the front.<P>However with the growing number of brake alterations cropping up, its entirely reasonable to see the Primary section of the MC changed to feeding the front brakes. In that case the odd flow patterns in the control valve could cause the Si to aerate and kill the front brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||06-29-2001||09:43 AM||||Dot 5 silicone brake fluid is only good for show cars. It is otherwise useless. Even though it can withstand higher temps, it does not abosorb moisture like regular brake fluid and so will go spongy very quickly in service. It is also critical to very thoroughly flush the brake system to eliminate all traces of normal brake fluid before you switch. The main benefit of it is that it will not damage painted surfaces. For improved brake performance at a good price, you can use Ford spec dot 3 fluid. It has a 550* boiling point as compared to 450-480 for regular Dot 3 and Dot 4 fluids.||||reg||1
